<Layout title="Our modelling manifesto" description="A context-first modelling manifesto: practitioner language, evidence, explicit identity, connected local models, reusable standards and human authority over meaning.">
  <h1>Our modelling manifesto</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Shared meaning should grow from the people who understand the work. We begin with
    their questions, language and evidence, build precise models within accountable
    communities, and connect those models through explicit agreements.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the approach we follow for the Smart Property Data Trust Framework.
    It brings domain-driven design, linked data and careful ontology engineering
    together around a practical aim: make property information understandable and
    reusable across the people and systems that need it. The
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision guide</a> distinguishes these
    normative source ODRs, the OPDA scope and the capabilities of today's candidates.
  </p>

  <h2 id="start-with-work">1. Start with work, questions and evidence</h2>
  <p>
    Before drawing a hierarchy, ask what someone needs to understand or decide.
    Which property does this inspection concern? Who made this statement? Which
    definition of completion does this workflow use? What evidence would make a
    proposed definition wrong? These questions give every important modelling
    distinction a purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Existing forms, schemas, spreadsheets and applications are valuable evidence.
    They show what people record and where systems struggle. They do not automatically
    determine what exists in the domain: a nested JSON object need not be a new thing,
    and a missing field does not prove a business concept is unnecessary.
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-method#questions">Competency questions</a>
    connect the model to the work it must support.
  </p>

  <h2 id="local-authority">2. Let meaning have an accountable home</h2>
  <p>
    Conveyancers, surveyors and lenders may use the same word with different,
    legitimate meanings. Asking one central model to settle those meanings before
    the communities have examined them risks losing the distinctions the industry
    needs. A bounded context gives a language, a model and responsibility for change
    an explicit home.
  </p>
  <p>
    Contexts are discovered through meaning and authority. They are not automatically
    one per application, department, database or working group. Start with the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/bounded-contexts">domain-group boundaries</a>, then
    test them against real disagreements and shared decisions. A boundary is useful
    when it helps people say precisely what they mean and who can change it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="connect-without-collapse">3. Connect models without erasing their differences</h2>
  <p>
    Interoperability does not require every community to use one enormous model.
    It requires enough shared meaning to exchange information safely and enough
    explicit mapping to explain the differences that remain. We favour a small
    common agreement, context-owned meanings and reviewable bridges between them.
  </p>
  <p>
    A title, building, parcel and transaction record can refer to a related property
    situation without being interchangeable things. Reusing a label or identifier is
    not proof of identity. A good mapping states its direction, purpose, evidence
    and limitations; sometimes the correct outcome is no mapping. Strategic
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/context-maps">context maps</a> explain the relationship
    between communities, while term mappings explain particular correspondences.
  </p>

  <h2 id="distinctions">4. Make important distinctions explicit</h2>
  <p>
    What something is, how it is classified, the role it plays and the phase it is
    in are different questions. A person can sell one home while buying another.
    A document can serve as evidence for a claim. An application can move through
    several states without becoming a different application every time.
  </p>
  <p>
    We distinguish <a href="/semantic-modelling/identity-roles-and-phases">identity,
    <code>isA</code>, <code>roleOf</code> and <code>phaseOf</code></a> before selecting
    a representation. Controlled values usually belong in governed vocabularies,
    not a subclass for every code. A qualified relationship carries its own dates,
    participants or evidence when the business needs that detail.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reuse">5. Compose established languages by purpose</h2>
  <p>
    We do not need to invent a new language for labels, provenance, time or validation
    whenever a property context needs one. RDF provides a common graph substrate;
    SKOS organises concepts and controlled values; SHACL expresses testable expectations.
    Other vocabularies can contribute precisely scoped capabilities.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reuse is a judgement about meaning, ownership and dependencies, not a race to
    accumulate prefixes. Use an external term when it fits; map when meanings differ;
    create a local term when a real gap remains. Read the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/linked-data-languages">language-by-purpose guide</a>
    alongside the <a href="/semantic-modelling/standards">actual standards profile</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="foundations">6. Use foundational analysis to ask better questions</h2>
  <p>
    Foundational ontology offers useful questions about identity, dependence, change,
    events and relationships. We use that discipline to challenge weak models rather
    than making every business concept inherit from a universal external hierarchy.
    Practitioners should be able to understand the reason for a distinction without
    learning the foundational vocabulary first.
  </p>
  <p>
    A top-down design can be valuable where a well-understood, shared domain warrants
    it. Our starting point is different: local evidence and working language come
    first; common structure earns its place through demonstrated need. This is a
    deliberate combination of established methods, not a claim that RDF or OWL
    themselves require one particular design philosophy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="separate-concerns">7. Separate meaning, checks and delivery</h2>
  <p>
    A statement about the world, a rule for a particular exchange and a screen used
    to review that statement have different jobs. A lender may require information
    that an early estate-agency listing does not yet contain. That requirement can
    belong in a delivery profile without redefining the property itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Declare which inference rules run and which SHACL shapes are checked. Preserve
    the published semantics of the languages used. Generate forms, JSON, APIs and
    documentation as views or projections of the reviewed agreement, and check what
    those projections retain or lose. A passing validator is evidence of encoded
    conformance, not proof that a source claim is true.
  </p>

  <h2 id="participation">8. Make participation a normal professional activity</h2>
  <p>
    People should contribute by reading definitions, correcting examples, explaining
    exceptions and following relationships on the website. They should not need an
    ontology editor or a training course in RDF. A useful challenge such as “this
    also happens when there is no registered title” is modelling input of the highest
    value.
  </p>
  <p>
    Technical specialists maintain the encoding. Tools can draft, compare and check;
    they cannot supply the authority of the practitioners who review meaning.
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/taking-part">The participation guide</a> shows how
    to make a useful contribution through the pages and working groups.
  </p>

  <h2 id="evolve">9. Treat disagreement and change as part of the model</h2>
  <p>
    Preserve the source, version, rationale and owner of a decision. Record uncertainty
    and dissent. Make an alternative interpretation inspectable instead of smoothing
    it away in a generic definition. When a source or business practice changes,
    those records explain which terms, mappings and consumers need another look.
  </p>
  <p>
    This approach costs thought and coordination. A small shared model is still
    governed; local autonomy still needs shared contracts. The benefit we seek is
    that change can be discussed and assessed where it matters, without rebuilding
    the entire industry's vocabulary around every new requirement.
  </p>

  <h2 id="success">10. Judge success by what people can do with it</h2>
  <p>
    The number of classes is not the goal. Better questions are whether a practitioner
    can find and challenge a definition, an implementer can understand a boundary,
    a source can be traced, and two consumers can exchange information with its
    meaning intact. These are the reasons for making the model public and readable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Explore the <a href="/semantic-modelling/benefits">practical benefits</a>, follow
    the <a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-method">working method</a>, or
    <a href="/join">join a working group</a> to help shape the agreement.
  </p>
</Layout>
